WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.750
Production on the long-gestating sequel to
World War Z has now been halted indefinitely,

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.809
leaving audiences to wonder just how this
huge Brad Pitt hit ended up getting stuck

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:10.840
in studio limbo.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.060
Here are a few reasons why the world may never
get to see World War Z 2.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.180
As the old adage goes, you’ve got to spend
money to make money — but just try telling

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.000
that to Paramount.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:25.860
The Playlist reports that, although it wasn't
exactly the only issue, the astronomical budget

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.220
of World War Z 2 absolutely played a role
in the film's demise.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.300
Considering the costs of special effects,
shooting locations, and cast salaries, it's

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.140
easy to see how the budget for a sequel of
this magnitude could skyrocket.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.670
Reportedly, director David Fincher's team
had been staffing up for a lengthy shoot,

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.489
which was to take place across five different
countries — as well as a six-month stint

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:45.180
in Atlanta.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:49.480
Even for a blockbuster project like this,
that's a lot of travel for a production to

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:49.980
endure.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.420
In comparison, Thor: The Dark World, the sequel
to 2011's Thor, was almost entirely shot in

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.360
Iceland and England, landing it with a budget
of $170 million.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.520
“If it were easy, everyone would do it.”

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:07.320
When you think about just how much World War
Z 2 might have cost, it’s no wonder that

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.340
Paramount got cold feet.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.900
Brad Pitt is arguably one of the most respected
actors in Hollywood, so it should come as

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.840
no surprise to find that he's typically in
very high demand.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:21.860
Between 2004 and 2017, he starred in at least
one film every year, while also doing a lot

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.880
of work with his own production company, Plan
B Entertainment.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.480
“And cousin, business is a-boomin’”

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.040
Although Pitt has been a major supporter for
a World War Z sequel, his busy schedule hasn't

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.060
exactly made it easy on the film’s production.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:39.540
For a good part of 2018, he was shooting Quentin
Tarantino's Once Upon a Time in Hollywood,

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:43.710
and although filming has since wrapped, that’s
just one example of how Pitt's ever-changing

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.500
agenda could make it difficult for Paramount
to get anything solid on the books for World

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.500
War Z 2.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:55.640
In 2018, Disney/Pixar released the long-anticipated
sequel for their 2004 hit, The Incredibles.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.820
It took the creative team an astounding 14
years to finally crank out a new story for

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.140
the Parr family and their superhero antics,
but it ultimately proved to be worth the wait.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.380
Although the long wait between the first and
second film worked in that particular case,

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.000
it should be considered the exception, not
the rule.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.360
A sequel to World War Z would've made the
most sense if was released two to three years

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.380
after the original.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.920
By waiting so long, many fans' memories of
the film have begun to fade, and with it the

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.300
anticipation of a follow-up.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.380
At one time, director J.A. Bayona was attached
to the sequel, but Universal ended up luring

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.920
him away to make Jurassic World: Fallen Kingdom
instead.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.260
At this point, it's been more than five years
since World War Z hit theaters, and you have

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.980
to wonder if that's simply too long of a gap
for audiences to really to care about what

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.620
happened to Gerry Lane and his family.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.240
If you ask three friends what they thought
of World War Z, you'll probably get three

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.320
different answers.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.420
Although many of the critics' reviews were
generally positive, the film only received

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.040
a 66% on Rotten Tomatoes, with a 72% audience
score.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:57.240
While that's by no means a negative reaction,
you have to assume that Paramount was hoping

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:02.040
for better marks on a film whose budget ended
up being around $200 million.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.660
Since reviews are relative, it's tricky to
accurately pinpoint a film's popularity using

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.300
that scale.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:11.520
Perhaps the best way to find a consensus is
to hold it up against other films with similar

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.020
budgets.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:18.240
For comparison, Titanic had a budget of $200
million, and received an 89% on Rotten Tomatoes;

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.800
while Peter Jackson's King Kong had a budget
of $207 million, and received an 84%.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.040
Avatar had a budget of $237 million, and received
an 82%.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.260
Considering that each of those films earned
scores higher than 80%, you could make the

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.420
case that World War Z didn't reach its highest
critical potential.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.700
It’s also entirely possible that, after
years of false starts on production, this

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.700
played a role in Paramount's decision to shut
down production on World War Z 2.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:51.040
After J.A. Bayona left the project in 2017,
Brad Pitt pushed for his long-time collaborator,

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.500
David Fincher, to take the reins on World
War Z 2.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:58.170
Together, the duo's impressive resume includes
Se7en, Fight Club, and The Curious Case of

00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:59.060
Benjamin Button.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:02.740
Once it became official that Fincher was on
board, all signs suggested that the film was

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.200
finally gaining some traction.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.420
However, that was not to be the case.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.660
UPROXX reported that, while waiting for Paramount
to get their stuff together, Fincher "went

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.310
and oversaw two seasons of the Netflix show
Mindhunter, some of which he directed personally."

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:19.790
Although he hasn't directed a film since 2015's
Gone Girl, Fincher has managed to stay busy

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:20.989
as a producer, too.

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:24.690
And this isn't the first time he's been linked
to a major project that never happened, as

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:29.850
he was also attached to Disney's rumored 20,000
Leagues Under the Sea project in 2014.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.510
Since this has now happened twice to the same
director, it makes you wonder if planning

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:37.600
around Fincher's busy schedule was what made
it difficult for World War Z 2 to get going.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.080
In October 2010, The Walking Dead premiered,
pushing stories about the undead back into

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.350
the pop culture spotlight.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.800
The zombie craze might have hit its apex in
2013, influencing video games like Call of

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.190
Duty: Black Ops and Red Dead Redemption, as
well as several movies, including Warm Bodies

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.540
and World War Z itself.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.400
Back then, everyone seemed more than happy
to prepare for the zombie apocalypse.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:00.120
“Don’t say that

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:01.400
What?

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.180
That.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:03.260
What?

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.220
The dead word.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:06.620
Why not?

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.180
Because it’s ridiculous.”

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:10.900
But that was years ago, and times have changed.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:15.330
The once-strong heartbeat of zombie popularity
is now little more than a flatline, and it

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.840
really does seem like zombie mania has long
since peaked.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.930
Viewing figures on The Walking Dead are dwindling,
while the 2018 zombie thriller Overlord brought

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:28.480
in a measly $41.7 million at the box office,
despite a positive critical reception.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:33.020
Who's to say that World War Z 2 wouldn't suffer
a similar fate in such a zombie-hostile environment?

